> I gotta disagree, here. Speed boots boost you up another notch to
> 'very fast' which I find highly useful.

It's really not that much of a difference. And if you have any sort of
luck, you'll find or polypile a spellbook of haste self at some time
anyway - wizards and priestesses can get by quite well just relying on
that spell, even without _any_ permanent intrinsic speed. 

> A helm of brilliance is a good bet for a wiz, too, since it's a hard
> helmet that doesn't screw up spellcasting.

Helmets don't hamper spellcasting very much, and wizards don't have
too much use for extra smartness. Nice, but not worth a wish for me.
Once i have magic-protection and reflection, i'd go for magic markers
all the way. 

I must admit that for me there are only two things definitely worth a
wish - magic-protection and candles; obviously only if there's no
other way visible to procure them when they're needed. Level 2 of the
Dungeons is a mite early for wishing for candles, though ...

Of course, for the OP, the best thing to wish for would have been
moderation - the character clearly got killed by playing with
fountains one time too many in the very early game (i'd say at least
three times too many, mind you <g>).

